window.docContents[176].resolve({"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___1___-Subrepresentations-in-semisimple-representations/Classification-of-subrepresentations-in-semisimple-representations/#Chapter3___Proposition3___1___4":{"contents":"Proposition 3.1.4. Let V_i, 1 \\leq i \\leq m, be irreducible finite dimensional pairwise nonisomorphic representations of A, and let W be a subrepresentation of V = \\bigoplus_{i=1}^m n_i V_i. Then W is isomorphic to \\bigoplus_{i=1}^m r_i V_i, r_i \\leq n_i, and the inclusion \\phi : W \\to V is a direct sum of inclusions \\phi_i : r_i V_i \\to n_i V_i given by multiplication of a row vector of elements of V_i (of length r_i) by a certain r_i \\times n_i matrix X_i with linearly independent rows: \\phi(v_1, \\ldots, v_{r_i}) = (v_1, \\ldots, v_{r_i}) X_i.\n\nProof. The proof is by induction in n := \\sum_{i=1}^m n_i. The base of induction (n = 1) is clear. To perform the induction step, let us assume that W is nonzero, and fix an irreducible subrepresentation P \\subset W. Such P exists (Problem 2.3.15).Another proof of the existence of P, which does not use the finite dimensionality of V, is by induction in n. Namely, if W itself is not irreducible, let K be the kernel of the projection of W to the first summand V_1. Then K is a subrepresentation of (n_1 - 1)V_1 \\oplus \\cdots \\oplus n_m V_m, which is nonzero since W is not irreducible, so K contains an irreducible subrepresentation by the induction assumption. Now, by Schur's lemma, P is isomorphic to V_i for some i, and the inclusion \\phi|_P : P \\to V factors through n_i V_i and upon identification of P with V_i is given by the formula v \\mapsto (vq_1, \\ldots, vq_{n_i}), where q_l \\in k are not all zero.\n\nNow note that the group G_i = GL_{n_i}(k) of invertible n_i \\times n_i matrices over k acts on n_i V_i by (v_1, \\ldots, v_{n_i}) \\mapsto (v_1, \\ldots, v_{n_i}) g_i (and by the identity on n_j V_j, j \\neq i) and therefore acts on the set of subrepresentations of V, preserving the property we need to establish: namely, under the action of g_i, the matrix X_i goes to X_i g_i, while the matrices X_j, j \\neq i, don't change. Take g_i \\in G_i such that (q_1, \\ldots, q_{n_i}) g_i = (1, 0, \\ldots, 0). Then Wg_i contains the first summand V_i of n_i V_i (namely, it is Pg_i); hence Wg_i = V_i \\oplus W', where W' \\subset n_1 V_1 \\oplus \\cdots \\oplus (n_i - 1) V_i \\oplus \\cdots \\oplus n_m V_m is the kernel of the projection of Wg_i to the first summand V_i along the other summands. Thus the required statement follows from the induction assumption. \\square\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00093. General results of representation theory\u00093.1. Subrepresentations in semisimple representations\u0009Classification of subrepresentations in semisimple representations","header":"Classification of subrepresentations in semisimple representations","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___1___-Subrepresentations-in-semisimple-representations/Classification-of-subrepresentations-in-semisimple-representations/#Chapter3___Proposition3___1___4"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___12___-Representations-of-___S_n___/All-irreducible-representations-of-S_n-can-be-given-by-matrices-with-rational-entries/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___12___-Representations-of-___S_n___--All-irreducible-representations-of-S_n-can-be-given-by-matrices-with-rational-entries--All-irreducible-representations-of-S_n-can-be-given-by-matrices-with-rational-entries--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"Every simple module over the displayed algebra admits a partition-indexed model whose\nrational permutation module is simple and whose complex scalar extension intertwines the\npermutation action.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Corollary5.12.4; role=supporting]\n\nFor each partition, there exists a map from the complex scalar extension of the associated\nrational module that intertwines the permutation action.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Corollary5.12.4; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.12. Representations of $S_n$\u0009All irreducible representations of S_n can be given by matrices with rational entries\u0009All irreducible representations of S_n can be given by matrices with rational entries\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___12___-Representations-of-___S_n___/All-irreducible-representations-of-S_n-can-be-given-by-matrices-with-rational-entries/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___12___-Representations-of-___S_n___--All-irreducible-representations-of-S_n-can-be-given-by-matrices-with-rational-entries--All-irreducible-representations-of-S_n-can-be-given-by-matrices-with-rational-entries--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___15___-The-Frobenius-character-formula/Section-5___15___-The-Frobenius-character-formula/#Chapter5___Introduction_5___15":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.15. The Frobenius character formula\u0009Section 5.15: The Frobenius character formula","header":"Section 5.15: The Frobenius character formula","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___15___-The-Frobenius-character-formula/Section-5___15___-The-Frobenius-character-formula/#Chapter5___Introduction_5___15"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___17___-The-hook-length-formula/#chapter-05___section-5-17":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results","header":"5.17. The hook length formula","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___17___-The-hook-length-formula/#chapter-05___section-5-17"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___18___-Schur-Weyl-duality-for-______mathfrak___gl____LPAR_V_RPAR____/Double-Centralizer-Theorem/#Chapter5___Theorem5___18___1___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.18. Schur-Weyl duality for $\\mathfrak{gl}(V)$\u0009Double Centralizer Theorem\u0009Double Centralizer Theorem","header":"Formalization","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___18___-Schur-Weyl-duality-for-______mathfrak___gl____LPAR_V_RPAR____/Double-Centralizer-Theorem/#Chapter5___Theorem5___18___1___formalization"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___19___-Schur-Weyl-duality-for-___GL_LPAR_V_RPAR____/Image-of-GL_LPAR_V_RPAR_-in-End_LPAR_V_________n____RPAR_-spans-B/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___19___-Schur-Weyl-duality-for-___GL_LPAR_V_RPAR____--Image-of-GL_LPAR_V_RPAR_-in-End_LPAR_V_________n____RPAR_-spans-B--Image-of-GL_LPAR_V_RPAR_-in-End_LPAR_V_________n____RPAR_-spans-B--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"Over an infinite field, the span of the range of the pointwise tensor-product maps induced\nby linear equivalences is the underlying submodule of the auxiliary subalgebra.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Proposition5.19.1; role=primary]\n\nOver a field of characteristic zero, the span of the range of the pointwise tensor-product\nmaps induced by linear equivalences is the underlying submodule of the centralizer of the\nauxiliary set.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Proposition5.19.1; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.19. Schur-Weyl duality for $GL(V)$\u0009Image of GL(V) in End(V^{⊗n}) spans B\u0009Image of GL(V) in End(V^{⊗n}) spans B\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___19___-Schur-Weyl-duality-for-___GL_LPAR_V_RPAR____/Image-of-GL_LPAR_V_RPAR_-in-End_LPAR_V_________n____RPAR_-spans-B/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___19___-Schur-Weyl-duality-for-___GL_LPAR_V_RPAR____--Image-of-GL_LPAR_V_RPAR_-in-End_LPAR_V_________n____RPAR_-spans-B--Image-of-GL_LPAR_V_RPAR_-in-End_LPAR_V_________n____RPAR_-spans-B--Formalization--Primary-declarations"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___22___-The-characters-of-___L____lambda___/Irreducible-representations-of-GL_LPAR_V_RPAR_-labeled-by-Young-diagrams/#Chapter5___Discussion_after_Theorem5___22___1___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.22. The characters of $L_\\lambda$\u0009Irreducible representations of GL(V) labeled by Young diagrams\u0009Irreducible representations of GL(V) labeled by Young diagrams","header":"Formalization","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___22___-The-characters-of-___L____lambda___/Irreducible-representations-of-GL_LPAR_V_RPAR_-labeled-by-Young-diagrams/#Chapter5___Discussion_after_Theorem5___22___1___formalization"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___3___-Frobenius-divisibility/Completion-of-proof-of-Theorem-5___3___1-_LPAR_Frobenius-divisibility_RPAR_/#Chapter5___Discussion_proof_of_Theorem5___3___1":{"contents":"Now, consider\n\n\\sum_i \\lambda_i \\overline{\\chi_V(g_{C_i})}.\n\nThis is an algebraic integer, since:\n\n(i) \\lambda_i are algebraic integers by Proposition 5.3.2,\n\n(ii) \\overline{\\chi_V(g_{C_i})} is a sum of roots of unity (it is the sum of eigenvalues of the matrix of \\rho(g_{C_i}), and since g_{C_i}^{|G|} = e in G, the eigenvalues of \\rho(g_{C_i}) are roots of unity), and\n\n(iii) \\overline{\\mathbb{Z}} is a ring (Proposition 5.2.4).\n\nOn the other hand, from the definition of \\lambda_i,\n\n\\sum_{C_i} \\lambda_i \\overline{\\chi_V(g_{C_i})} = \\sum_i \\frac{|C_i| \\chi_V(g_{C_i}) \\overline{\\chi_V(g_{C_i})}}{\\dim V}.\n\nRecalling that \\chi_V is a class function, this is equal to\n\n\\sum_{g \\in G} \\frac{\\chi_V(g) \\overline{\\chi_V(g)}}{\\dim V} = \\frac{|G|(\\chi_V, \\chi_V)}{\\dim V}.\n\nSince V is an irreducible representation, (\\chi_V, \\chi_V) = 1, so\n\n\\sum_{C_i} \\lambda_i \\overline{\\chi_V(g_{C_i})} = \\frac{|G|}{\\dim V}.\nSince \\frac{|G|}{\\dim V} \\in \\mathbb{Q} and \\sum_{C_i} \\lambda_i \\overline{\\chi_V(g_{C_i})} \\in \\overline{\\mathbb{Z}}, by Proposition 5.2.5, \\frac{|G|}{\\dim V} \\in \\mathbb{Z}. \\square\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.3. Frobenius divisibility\u0009Completion of proof of Theorem 5.3.1 (Frobenius divisibility)","header":"Completion of proof of Theorem 5.3.1 (Frobenius divisibility)","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___3___-Frobenius-divisibility/Completion-of-proof-of-Theorem-5___3___1-_LPAR_Frobenius-divisibility_RPAR_/#Chapter5___Discussion_proof_of_Theorem5___3___1"},"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___2___-Indecomposable-representations-of-the-quivers-___A_1______-___A_2______-___A_3___/Example-6___2___2___-Indecomposable-representations-of-A1/#Chapter6___Example6___2___2":{"contents":"Example 6.2.2 (A_1). The quiver A_1 consists of a single vertex and has no edges. Since a representation of this quiver is just a single vector space, the only indecomposable representation is the ground field (= a 1-dimensional space).\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00096. Quiver representations\u00096.2. Indecomposable representations of the quivers $A_1$, $A_2$, $A_3$\u0009Example 6.2.2: Indecomposable representations of A1","header":"Example 6.2.2: Indecomposable representations of A1","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___2___-Indecomposable-representations-of-the-quivers-___A_1______-___A_2______-___A_3___/Example-6___2___2___-Indecomposable-representations-of-A1/#Chapter6___Example6___2___2"},"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___8___-Proof-of-Gabriel___s-theorem/Corollary-6___8___2___-Dimension-vector-of-indecomposable-is-a-positive-root/#Chapter6___Corollary6___8___2___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00096. Quiver representations\u00096.8. Proof of Gabriel's theorem\u0009Corollary 6.8.2: Dimension vector of indecomposable is a positive root\u0009Corollary 6.8.2: Dimension vector of indecomposable is a positive root","header":"Formalization","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___8___-Proof-of-Gabriel___s-theorem/Corollary-6___8___2___-Dimension-vector-of-indecomposable-is-a-positive-root/#Chapter6___Corollary6___8___2___formalization"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___2___-Projective-covers/Classification-of-indecomposable-projective-modules/#Chapter9___Theorem9___2___1":{"contents":"Theorem 9.2.1. (i) For each i = 1, \\ldots, n there exists a unique indecomposable finitely generated projective module P_i such that\n\n\n\\dim \\operatorname{Hom}(P_i, M_j) = \\delta_{ij}.\n\n(ii) A = \\bigoplus_{i=1}^n (\\dim M_i) P_i.\n\n(iii) Any indecomposable finitely generated projective module over A is isomorphic to P_i for some i.\n\nProof. Recall that A / \\operatorname{Rad}(A) = \\bigoplus_{i=1}^n \\operatorname{End}(M_i) and that \\operatorname{Rad}(A) is a nilpotent ideal. Pick a basis of M_i, and let e_{ij}^0 = E_{jj}^i, the rank 1 projectors projecting to the basis vectors of this basis (j = 1, \\ldots, \\dim M_i). Then e_{ij}^0 are orthogonal idempotents in A / \\operatorname{Rad}(A). So by Corollary 9.1.3 we can lift them to orthogonal idempotents e_{ij} in A. Now define P_{ij} = Ae_{ij}. Then A = \\bigoplus_i \\bigoplus_{j=1}^{\\dim M_i} P_{ij}, so P_{ij} are projective. Also, we have \\operatorname{Hom}(P_{ij}, M_k) = e_{ij} M_k, so \\dim \\operatorname{Hom}(P_{ij}, M_k) = \\delta_{ik}. Finally, P_{ij} is independent of j up to an isomorphism, as e_{ij} for fixed i are conjugate under A^\\times by Proposition 9.1.1; thus we will denote P_{ij} by P_i.\n\nWe claim that P_i is indecomposable. Indeed, if P_i = Q_1 \\oplus Q_2, then \\operatorname{Hom}(Q_l, M_j) = 0 for all j either for l = 1 or for l = 2, so either Q_1 = 0 or Q_2 = 0.\n\nAlso, there can be no other indecomposable finitely generated projective modules, since any indecomposable projective module has to occur in the decomposition of A. The theorem is proved. \\square\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00099. Structure of finite dimensional algebras\u00099.2. Projective covers\u0009Classification of indecomposable projective modules","header":"Classification of indecomposable projective modules","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___2___-Projective-covers/Classification-of-indecomposable-projective-modules/#Chapter9___Theorem9___2___1"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___4___-Homological-dimension/Homological-dimension-of-polynomial-algebra-_LPAR_Hilbert-syzygies-theorem_RPAR_/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras--9___4___-Homological-dimension--Homological-dimension-of-polynomial-algebra-_LPAR_Hilbert-syzygies-theorem_RPAR_--Homological-dimension-of-polynomial-algebra-_LPAR_Hilbert-syzygies-theorem_RPAR_--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"A multivariate polynomial ring indexed by Fin n over a field satisfies the auxiliary property at index n.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Example9.4.4; role=supporting]\n\nA ring equivalence from R to S transfers the auxiliary property at the same index from S to R.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Example9.4.4; role=supporting]\n\nFor a small commutative ring, the auxiliary property at index d implies the property at index d + 1 for its polynomial ring.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Example9.4.4; role=supporting]\n\nA small semisimple ring satisfies the auxiliary property in degree zero.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Example9.4.4; role=supporting]\n\nIf a multivariate polynomial ring indexed by Fin n over a field has the auxiliary property at index d, then n is at most d.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Example9.4.4; role=supporting]\n\nAn equivalence of abelian categories preserves the bound on projective dimension.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Example9.4.4; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00099. Structure of finite dimensional algebras\u00099.4. Homological dimension\u0009Homological dimension of polynomial algebra (Hilbert syzygies theorem)\u0009Homological dimension of polynomial algebra (Hilbert syzygies theorem)\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___4___-Homological-dimension/Homological-dimension-of-polynomial-algebra-_LPAR_Hilbert-syzygies-theorem_RPAR_/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras--9___4___-Homological-dimension--Homological-dimension-of-polynomial-algebra-_LPAR_Hilbert-syzygies-theorem_RPAR_--Homological-dimension-of-polynomial-algebra-_LPAR_Hilbert-syzygies-theorem_RPAR_--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"}});